      <div class="limits__grid">
        <article class="limits__item"><h3>You run one agent at a time</h3><p>Start a session, watch it, finish. A workspace is overhead. Everything here is in service of the case where three or four are running and you have lost track of which one is waiting on you.</p></article>
        <article class="limits__item"><h3>You want work to continue after you close the lid</h3><p>It will not. The agents run on your machine, so they stop when it sleeps. If that is the requirement, a product with cloud workspaces is the right shape, and we say which ones on the <a href="/compare/claude-code-guis">comparison page</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="limits__item"><h3>You wanted an editor</h3><p>Topics is not an IDE and is not becoming one. It hosts the terminal agents and the surfaces around them; you keep whatever editor you already use.</p></article>
        <article class="limits__item"><h3>You need accounts, roles or a shared board</h3><p>There is no authentication layer at all. Boards sync between <em>your</em> machines. Exposing one over a tunnel means putting your own auth in front of it.</p></article>
        <article class="limits__item"><h3>macOS will say the developer cannot be verified</h3><p>The build is signed ad-hoc, so Gatekeeper stops it on first launch and since Sequoia the control-click bypass is gone. <a href="#download">The three steps are below</a>, and if that is a dealbreaker it is a fair one.</p></article>
